title: That Ye Might Believe: The Gospel of John
description: A chapter-by-chapter study of John's figures, dark sayings and dual phrasings, and the one thing they all restate: believe on the Son.
output: lessons/bible/john-that-ye-might-believe.html
highlight: believ

# Rebuilt on the deck template from the original study, slide for slide. The chapter slides follow one pattern:
# the figure, the literal hearer, the revealed meaning. Verses are pulled from the KJV and shown as blocks.

=== Introduction
--- That Ye Might Believe [title]
overline: The Gospel of John
sub: The Father gave His Son, who died, was buried and rose again. Whosoever believeth on Him hath everlasting life. Traced chapter by chapter through the figures, dark sayings and dual phrasings of the Gospel of John.
big: John 3:16
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse. The Contents button lists every chapter.


--- Two devices to watch for
lead: In our study of the Gospel of John, we will meet two hallmark literary devices that the author frequently wraps around the teachings of Jesus to unlock deep spiritual truths.
panel: 1. The Johannine Misunderstanding Motif | A rhetorical strategy widely recognized by scholars, often triggered by *amphibologia*, or words with double meanings. Jesus delivers a profound spiritual truth or “dark saying,” a hearer mistakenly takes it in a strictly physical, material way, and Jesus uses that error as a springboard to explain the deeper heavenly reality.
panel: 2. Semitic Parallelism | Specifically a blend of synonymous and antithetic parallelism. John uses poetic restatements to set two opposing realities side by side, such as the physical flesh versus the supernatural Spirit, to define and clarify his terms.
take: A note on where this came from: I noticed both patterns in my own study of John and built the first version of this lesson around them before I knew they had names. They are well known and widely taught. I did not invent them, and I am not claiming to have discovered them. I only found them in the text.

--- The gospel in one verse
lead: Before we follow the figures, we set down the whole message plainly. John 3:16 holds all of it.
panel: “For God so loved the world” | Salvation begins with the Father’s love and grace, not with anything in us ([[John 3:17]], [[Ephesians 2:8]]).
panel: “that he gave his only begotten Son” | The Son died for our sins, was buried and rose again the third day ([[1 Corinthians 15:3-4]], [[John 10:17-18]], [[John 19:30]]).
panel: “that whosoever believeth in him” | It is received by believing on Him, and it is offered to all ([[John 3:15]], [[John 12:32-33]]).
panel: “should not perish, but have everlasting life” | The believer is quickened, sealed by the Holy Spirit of promise and kept by the power of God ([[Ephesians 2:5]], [[Ephesians 1:13]], [[1 Peter 1:5]], [[John 10:28-29]]).
take: When John says “believe,” he means believing on this Son: God manifest in the flesh, given by the Father, crucified and risen. Every figure in the book restates this verse.

--- How John teaches
lead: Again and again in John, Jesus says something in a picture. Those who will not believe take the picture literally and are confused or offended. Then Jesus, or John himself, says it again plainly. The plain meaning is nearly always the same: believe on Him.
p: Every chapter in this study is laid out the same way:
fig: The figure |  | A picture Jesus uses: a temple, a birth, water, bread, a door, sleep.
lit: The literal hearer |  | Someone takes the picture at face value and misses the point, or is offended by it.
rev: The revealed meaning |  | The plain truth, given by Jesus or explained by John. Most often it is a call to believe on Him.
take: Jesus said it Himself: “These things have I spoken unto you in proverbs” ([[John 16:25]]).

--- Why speak in figures?
lead: The psalmist foretold that the Messiah would speak in parables and dark sayings, and the other gospels record Jesus explaining why.
scripture: Psalm 78:2
scripture: Matthew 13:13
p: A figure does not hide the truth from anyone who wants it. It shows who is listening in faith. The believer looks past the picture to the One speaking. The unbeliever argues with the picture and walks away. John records Jesus saying the same thing to those who opposed Him:
scripture: John 8:43
take: The believer hears that Christ’s words “are spirit, and they are life” ([[John 6:63]]).

--- Seven figures and the bare “I am”
lead: Two kinds of “I am” run through John. Seven times Jesus joins “I am” to a figure that describes who He is. Other times He says simply “I am,” the name God gave Moses at the burning bush. [[Exodus 3:14]]
p: **The seven figures**
tile: [[John 6:35]] | The bread of life
tile: [[John 8:12]] | The light of the world
tile: [[John 10:9]] | The door
tile: [[John 10:11]] | The good shepherd
tile: [[John 11:25]] | The resurrection, and the life
tile: [[John 14:6]] | The way, the truth, and the life
tile: [[John 15:1]] | The true vine
p: **“I am *he*”**
tile: [[John 4:26]] | To the woman at the well
tile: [[John 8:24]] | Believe, or die in your sins
tile: [[John 8:28]] | Known when He is lifted up
tile: [[John 8:58]] | Before Abraham was
tile: [[John 13:19]] | That ye may believe
tile: [[John 18:5-6]] | They fell to the ground

=== John 1

--- The Word Made Flesh
kicker: John 1
lead: John begins by telling us exactly who Jesus is, before a single miracle is recorded.
big: John 1:1
scripture: John 1:14
p: The Word was with God, and the Word was God. That same Word was made flesh and lived among men. The Son the Father gave is no lesser being. He is God, come in the flesh. Everything else in John rests on this.
take: No man hath seen God at any time. The only begotten Son has declared Him ([[John 1:18]]).

--- Received Him, Believed on Him
kicker: John 1
lead: In the same chapter John gives us the first figure, the first rejection and the first restatement.
fig: The figure | John 1:4-5 | John pictures Jesus as light shining into a dark world.
lit: The world’s response | John 1:10-11 | The world He made did not know Him, and His own people did not receive Him.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 1:12-13 | Then John says it plainly. “Received him” is restated as “believe on his name.” Those who believe are born of God, not by any human effort.
take: This is the pattern for the whole book: the figure, the response, and the plain meaning.

=== John 2

--- Destroy This Temple
kicker: John 2
lead: After Jesus drives the money changers out of the temple, the Jews demand a sign of His authority. He answers with a figure.
fig: The figure | John 2:19 | Jesus speaks of a temple destroyed and raised up in three days.
lit: The literal hearer | John 2:20 | His hearers think only of the stone building in front of them, which had taken forty-six years to build.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 2:21-22 | John tells us plainly that He meant His own body. After the resurrection, the disciples remembered His words and believed.
take: The very first figure in John points to His death and resurrection, the heart of the gospel.

=== John 3

--- Ye Must Be Born Again
kicker: John 3
lead: Nicodemus, a ruler of the Jews, comes to Jesus by night. Jesus answers him with a figure about birth.
fig: The figure | John 3:3 | To see the kingdom of God, a man must be born a second time.
lit: The literal hearer | John 3:4 | Nicodemus can only think of physical birth, and asks how a grown man could enter his mother’s womb again.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 3:5-6 | Jesus says it again two ways, in back-to-back verses. “Born of water” is restated as “born of the flesh”: our natural birth. “Born of the Spirit” is restated as “is spirit”: the second birth. Every person is born once of the flesh. He must be born again of the Spirit.
take: Like the wind, the new birth cannot be seen, but its effect is real ([[John 3:7-8]]).

--- As Moses Lifted Up the Serpent
kicker: John 3
lead: To explain how a man is born again, Jesus reaches back to a story Nicodemus knew well.
fig: The figure | Numbers 21:8-9 | When fiery serpents bit Israel in the wilderness, God had Moses set a brass serpent on a pole. Every bitten man who looked at it lived.
fig: The figure applied | John 3:14 | Jesus says He must be lifted up the same way, on the cross.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 3:15 | Looking at the serpent becomes believing on the Son. The bitten man did nothing but look. The sinner does nothing but believe.
take: “Look and live” in the wilderness becomes “believe and live” at the cross.

--- Said, Then Said Again
kicker: John 3
lead: Verse 16 repeats verse 15 almost word for word, then opens it wider to show where the gift came from.
parallel: John 3:15 · The serpent explained | John 3:16 · The gospel stated
row: (as the serpent was lifted up, 3:14) | For God so loved the world, that he gave his only begotten Son,
row: That whosoever believeth in him | that whosoever believeth in him
row: should not perish, | should not perish,
row: but have eternal life. | but have everlasting life.
p: “Eternal life” and “everlasting life” are the same gift. Verse 15 tells us what the serpent pictured. Verse 16 tells us why the Son was lifted up: the Father so loved the world that He gave Him.
scripture: John 3:18
take: The line is drawn by one thing only: believing, or not believing, on the Son.

=== John 4

--- Living Water
kicker: John 4
lead: At Jacob’s well, Jesus asks a Samaritan woman for a drink, then offers her water of a different kind.
fig: The figure | John 4:10 | Jesus speaks of living water that only He can give.
lit: The literal hearer | John 4:11-12 | She thinks of the well itself. He has no bucket, and the well is deep. Is He greater than Jacob, who dug it?
rev: The revealed meaning | John 4:13-14 | Jesus contrasts the two waters. Well water leaves you thirsty again. The water He gives satisfies once and for ever, springing up into everlasting life.
take: Even then she asks for it so she won’t have to come back and draw ([[John 4:15]]). John later explains the living water as the Spirit, received by those who believe ([[John 7:39]]).

--- Meat to Eat That Ye Know Not Of
kicker: John 4
lead: The disciples come back from town with food and urge Jesus to eat.
fig: The figure | John 4:32 | Jesus says He has food they do not know about.
lit: The literal hearer | John 4:33 | His own disciples wonder who brought Him something to eat.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 4:34 | His food is doing the will of the Father who sent Him and finishing the work He was given. That work is finished at the cross ([[John 19:30]]).
take: Even His disciples stumble at a figure until He explains it. This prepares us for John 6, where He Himself is the bread.

--- I That Speak unto Thee Am He
kicker: John 4
lead: The woman speaks of the coming Messiah, and Jesus answers her in plain words.
fig: Her hope | John 4:25 | She knows the Christ is coming and will tell them all things.
rev: The revelation | John 4:26 | This is the first time in John that Jesus says “I am.”
rev: The believers | John 4:41-42 | The Samaritans believe because of His own word and confess Him as the Saviour of the world.
take: She came for water from a well and went home believing on the Christ.

=== John 5

--- Equal with God
kicker: John 5
lead: Jesus heals a lame man on the sabbath, and the Jews seek to kill Him. His answer shows what kind of claim He was making.
fig: His words | John 5:17 | Jesus calls God “my Father” and places His own work alongside the Father’s.
lit: What they understood | John 5:18 | The unbelievers understood exactly what He meant. To call God His own Father in this way was to make Himself equal with God.
rev: The promise | John 5:24 | Then the plain promise: hear and believe, and you have everlasting life now. You will not come into condemnation, because you have already passed from death unto life.
take: Unbelief did not misunderstand His claim to be God. It rejected it.

=== John 6

--- I Am the Bread of Life
kicker: John 6
lead: The day after He fed the five thousand, the crowd comes looking for more bread ([[John 6:26]]). Jesus offers them Himself.
fig: The figure | John 6:35 | He calls Himself the bread of life and explains it in the same breath: coming to Him is eating, and believing on Him is drinking. One meal, and never hunger again.
lit: The literal hearer | John 6:52 | When He says the bread is His flesh, given for the life of the world ([[John 6:51]]), the Jews argue about how a man could give his flesh to eat. Many disciples call it a hard saying ([[John 6:60]]).
rev: The revealed meaning | John 6:63 | Jesus gives the key Himself. The flesh profits nothing. His words are spirit and life, received by believing them.
take: Peter understood: “thou hast the words of eternal life” ([[John 6:68-69]]).

--- Eating Is Believing
kicker: John 6
lead: Set verse 40 beside verse 54. Jesus states the same promise twice, once plainly and once in the figure.
parallel: John 6:40 · Plain words | John 6:54 · The figure
row: every one which seeth the Son, and believeth on him, | Whoso eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood,
row: may have everlasting life: | hath eternal life;
row: and I will raise him up at the last day. | and I will raise him up at the last day.
p: Same promise, same result. To eat His flesh and drink His blood is to believe on the Son. And the one who comes is kept:
scripture: John 6:37
scripture: John 6:39
take: None who come are cast out, and of all the Father has given Him, He will lose nothing.

=== John 7

--- Whither Will He Go?
kicker: John 7
lead: At the feast of tabernacles, the rulers send officers to take Jesus, and He speaks of going away.
fig: The figure | John 7:33-34 | He will soon go to the One who sent Him, where they cannot follow.
lit: The literal hearer | John 7:35-36 | The Jews think of a journey on the map, perhaps to teach the Jews scattered among the Gentiles.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 16:28 | Later He tells His disciples plainly: He came from the Father, and He is returning to the Father.
take: They thought of a trip abroad. He spoke of going back to heaven, by way of the cross and the empty tomb.

--- Rivers of Living Water
kicker: John 7
lead: On the last and greatest day of the feast, Jesus stands and cries out to the crowd.
fig: The figure | John 7:37-38 | He offers a drink to anyone who thirsts, and promises rivers flowing out of the one who believes.
rev: John’s explanation | John 7:39 | John stops to tell us what it means. The living water is the Holy Spirit, whom believers would receive once Jesus was glorified.
take: Coming and drinking is believing, and every believer receives the Spirit. This is the same water He offered the woman at the well.

=== John 8

--- Believe That I Am He
kicker: John 8
lead: Jesus has just declared, “I am the light of the world” ([[John 8:12]]). Again He speaks of going where they cannot follow.
fig: The figure | John 8:21 | He is going away, and they will die in their sins without Him.
lit: The literal hearer | John 8:22 | The Jews wonder if He means to kill Himself.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 8:23-24 | He explains: they are from beneath, and He is from above. The only way out of their sins is to believe that He is the I AM.
take: Here believing on Him and who He is are joined in a single sentence.

--- The Truth Shall Make You Free
kicker: John 8
lead: Jesus speaks to Jews who had begun to believe His words.
fig: The figure | John 8:31-32 | He speaks of being set free.
lit: The literal hearer | John 8:33 | They protest that Abraham’s children have never been anyone’s slaves.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 8:34-36 | The bondage is sin, and only the Son can set a man free. A servant can be put out of the house. The Son abides for ever, and the freedom He gives is freedom indeed.
take: They heard a question of ancestry. He spoke of the slavery of sin.

--- Before Abraham Was, I Am
kicker: John 8
lead: The argument turns to Abraham, and Jesus makes His plainest claim yet.
fig: The figure | John 8:56 | Abraham looked ahead in faith to the day of Christ and rejoiced.
lit: The literal hearer | John 8:57 | They hear only a question of age. He is not even fifty, so how could He have seen Abraham?
rev: The revealed meaning | John 8:58 | Jesus answers with the name God gave Moses at the burning bush: “I AM” ([[Exodus 3:14]]). He did not say “I was.” He has no beginning.
scripture: John 8:59
take: The unbelievers understood the claim to be God, and reached for stones.

=== John 9

--- Are We Blind Also?
kicker: John 9
lead: Jesus says, “I am the light of the world” ([[John 9:5]]), and gives sight to a man born blind. The Pharisees question the man and cast him out.
fig: The figure | John 9:39 | Jesus uses sight and blindness as a picture of who receives Him.
lit: The literal hearer | John 9:40 | The Pharisees take it as an insult to their eyesight.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 9:41 | Their sin remains, not because they cannot see, but because they claim to see while they reject the Light standing in front of them.
take: The man born blind received his sight. The men who said “We see” remained in darkness.

--- Lord, I Believe
kicker: John 9
lead: After he is cast out, Jesus finds the healed man and asks him one question.
scripture: John 9:35-38
p: He believed and worshipped Him, and Jesus accepted it. Worship belongs to God alone. Then compare how Jesus described the light in John 8 with how He said it again in John 12:
parallel: John 8:12 · The figure | John 12:46 · Said again
row: he that followeth me | whosoever believeth on me
row: shall not walk in darkness | should not abide in darkness
take: Following the light is believing on Him.

=== John 10

--- I Am the Door
kicker: John 10
lead: Jesus speaks of a sheepfold, its door, its shepherd and the thieves who climb in another way.
fig: The figure | John 10:1-3 | A picture every shepherd in Israel would know: one rightful way into the fold.
lit: The hearers | John 10:6 | John calls it a parable and tells us plainly that they did not understand it.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 10:7; John 10:9 | So Jesus says it again. He is the door. Whoever enters by Him is saved.
take: “Then said Jesus unto them again” is the dual pattern in John’s own words.

--- I Am the Good Shepherd
kicker: John 10
lead: The figure continues, and this time it points straight to the cross.
fig: The figure | John 10:11 | A good shepherd gives his own life for his sheep.
rev: His death and resurrection | John 10:17-18 | No one takes His life from Him. He lays it down Himself, and He takes it again.
rev: The promise | John 10:27-29 | His sheep hear, follow and receive eternal life. They shall never perish, held in the Son’s hand and in the Father’s hand.
take: The security of the sheep rests on the Shepherd who died and rose again.

--- I and My Father Are One
kicker: John 10
lead: At the feast of dedication, the Jews press Him to tell them plainly whether He is the Christ.
fig: His words | John 10:30 | He has just said that no man can pluck the sheep out of the Father’s hand. Now He says He and the Father are one.
lit: What they understood | John 10:31-33 | They state His claim in their own words: “thou, being a man, makest thyself God.”
take: For the second time they took up stones. Even His enemies said it plainly. They understood He claimed to be God. They would not believe it.

=== John 11

--- Lazarus Sleepeth
kicker: John 11
lead: Word comes that Lazarus of Bethany is sick. Jesus waits two days, then tells His disciples they are going back.
fig: The figure | John 11:11 | Jesus speaks of death as sleep.
lit: The literal hearer | John 11:12-13 | The disciples think Lazarus is resting and will get better. John notes their mistake.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 11:14 | So Jesus tells them plainly.
take: For the believer, death is called sleep because the Lord will wake him.

--- Believest Thou This?
kicker: John 11
lead: Martha meets Jesus outside Bethany. Her brother has been in the grave four days.
fig: The figure | John 11:25-26 | Two clauses, one promise. The believer who dies will live again. The believer who lives will never die. Then He asks her directly.
rev: The believer’s answer | John 11:27 | Martha confesses who He is before Lazarus walks out of the tomb. She believed first and saw the glory of God afterward ([[John 11:40]]).
rev: The proof | John 11:43-44 | The voice of the Son of God calls a dead man out of the grave.
take: Many believed on Him ([[John 11:45]]). From that day the rulers plotted His death ([[John 11:53]]). The same question comes to every hearer: believest thou this?

=== John 12

--- If I Be Lifted Up
kicker: John 12
lead: In His last public teaching, Jesus speaks once more of being lifted up.
fig: The figure | John 12:32 | Lifted up from the earth, He will draw all men to Himself.
rev: John’s explanation | John 12:33 | John tells us at once what “lifted up” meant: the kind of death He would die, raised up on a cross.
lit: The literal hearer | John 12:34 | The people cannot fit a Christ who dies into their belief that the Christ abides for ever. They understood He meant death, and stumbled at it.
take: This is the third time Jesus speaks of being lifted up ([[John 3:14]], [[John 8:28]]). Each time it means the cross. By His death He draws all men, and whosoever believeth on Him lives.

=== John 13

--- If I Wash Thee Not
kicker: John 13
lead: At the last supper Jesus rises, takes a towel and begins washing His disciples’ feet.
fig: The figure | John 13:8 | Peter refuses, and Jesus answers that without His washing Peter has no part with Him.
lit: The literal hearer | John 13:9 | Peter swings the other way and asks for a full bath.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 13:10-11 | One who has been washed is clean every whit and only needs his feet washed. The washing that makes a man clean happens once. Judas was the exception, because he never believed.
take: Later that night: “ye may believe that I am *he*” ([[John 13:19]]).

=== John 14

--- I Am the Way
kicker: John 14
lead: Jesus comforts His troubled disciples: “ye believe in God, believe also in me” ([[John 14:1]]). He is going to prepare a place for them ([[John 14:2-3]]).
fig: The figure | John 14:4 | He tells them they know where He is going and the way there.
lit: The literal hearer | John 14:5 | Thomas thinks of a road and a destination he cannot see.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 14:6 | The way is not a road. It is a Person. No one comes to the Father except through Him.
take: Notice the dual phrasing in verse 1: believe in God, believe also in Me.

--- Shew Us the Father
kicker: John 14
lead: Philip asks for the one thing he thinks would settle everything.
fig: The figure | John 14:7 | To know Jesus is to know the Father. They have already seen Him.
lit: The literal hearer | John 14:8 | Philip wants a visible sight of the Father.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 14:9 | To see the Son is to see the Father. There are few plainer statements of His deity in all of scripture.
take: And the promise to the believer: “because I live, ye shall live also” ([[John 14:19]]).

=== John 15

--- I Am the True Vine
kicker: John 15
lead: The last of the seven figures, spoken to the eleven on the way to Gethsemane.
fig: The figure | John 15:1 | Jesus is the vine, and the Father tends it.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 15:5 | A branch lives and bears fruit only by being joined to the vine. Without Him we can do nothing.
take: The theme of the vine is fruit. Read [[John 15:6]] in light of what John has already promised the believer: never cast out ([[John 6:37]]), never perish ([[John 10:28]]).

=== John 16

--- A Little While
kicker: John 16
lead: Jesus prepares His disciples for His death and resurrection, and they cannot follow what He means.
fig: The figure | John 16:16 | Soon they will not see Him, and soon after they will see Him again.
lit: The confused hearers | John 16:17-18 | Even the disciples ask one another what He can mean.
rev: The revealed meaning | John 16:20-22 | His death would bring them sorrow, like a woman in labour. His resurrection would turn it to a joy no man could take away.
take: “A little while” was three days: the cross, the grave and the risen Lord.

--- Now Speakest Thou Plainly
kicker: John 16
lead: Jesus names the way He has been teaching, and then speaks without a figure.
fig: In proverbs | John 16:25 | He says He has been speaking in proverbs, and promises to speak plainly.
rev: Plainly | John 16:28 | No figure at all: He came from the Father and returns to the Father.
rev: The believers | John 16:29-30 | The disciples recognize the plain speech and believe that He came forth from God.
take: Jesus Himself called His sayings proverbs. Reading His figures as figures is following His own description of how He taught.

=== John 17

--- Keep Through Thine Own Name
kicker: John 17
lead: On the night before the cross, Jesus prays to the Father for those the Father has given Him.
rev: Life eternal | John 17:3 | Eternal life is knowing the only true God and the Son He sent.
rev: Kept | John 17:11-12 | He asks the Father to keep them. None of them is lost except Judas, the son of perdition, who never believed.
rev: With Him | John 17:24 | His will is that every one the Father gave Him be with Him where He is.
take: The Son asked the Father to keep them, and the Father always hears the Son.

=== John 18

--- I Am He
kicker: John 18
lead: Judas leads a band of soldiers and officers into the garden with lanterns, torches and weapons.
scripture: John 18:4-6
p: At the words “I am,” armed men go backward and fall to the ground. Then He gives Himself up willingly, and asks only that His disciples be let go:
scripture: John 18:8
take: He was not taken by force. He laid down His life, just as He said ([[John 10:18]]).

=== John 19

--- It Is Finished
kicker: John 19
lead: Every figure has pointed here: the temple destroyed, the serpent lifted up, the bread given, the shepherd laying down His life.
big: John 19:30
p: The work the Father gave Him to do is complete ([[John 4:34]], [[John 17:4]]). Nothing is left for the sinner to add. Three days later the temple of His body was raised, as He had said.
scripture: 1 Corinthians 15:3-4
take: Christ died for our sins, was buried and rose again. This is the gospel the believer believes.

=== John 20

--- My Lord and My God
kicker: John 20
lead: Thomas was absent when the risen Lord first appeared to the disciples.
lit: The literal demand | John 20:25 | Thomas will believe only what he can see and touch.
rev: The revelation | John 20:27-28 | Eight days later, Thomas confesses the risen Jesus as Lord and God, exactly what John declared in the first verse.
rev: Said again, for us | John 20:29 | The last dual saying is for every reader who was not there: blessed are those who believe without seeing.
scripture: John 20:30-31
take: John tells us why he wrote the whole book: that we might believe, and that believing we might have life.

=== John 21

--- What Is That to Thee?
kicker: John 21
lead: By the sea of Tiberias, Peter asks the risen Lord what will happen to John.
fig: His words | John 21:22 | Jesus tells Peter that John’s future is not his concern. Peter is to follow.
lit: The literal hearers | John 21:23 | The brethren turned His words into a rumor that John would never die. John corrects it by repeating exactly what Jesus said, not what they assumed.
take: Even in the last chapter, John shows the difference between reading what the Lord said and reading into it.

=== Pulling it together

--- Believe, and live
lead: Looking back over the whole book, the only work John ever names is believing. He states the condition for life again and again, and never adds anything to it.
xr: John 6:28-29 | “This is the work of God, that ye believe on him whom he hath sent.”
xr: John 1:12 | Sons of God by believing on His name.
xr: John 3:16 | Whosoever believeth should not perish.
xr: John 5:24 | Heareth and believeth, hath everlasting life, and is passed from death unto life.
xr: John 6:47 | He that believeth on me hath everlasting life.
xr: John 11:25-26 | He that believeth shall live and never die.
xr: John 20:31 | Believing, ye might have life through His name.
take: Condemnation in John rests on one thing: “because he hath not believed” ([[John 3:18]]).

--- Kept for ever
lead: The believer’s security is not a single proof text. John repeats it across the whole gospel in five kinds of promise.
group: Never | never thirst, never hunger, never see death, never perish, never die | John 4:14; John 6:35; John 8:51; John 10:28; John 11:26
group: Hath now | everlasting life possessed today, condemnation already past | John 3:36; John 5:24; John 6:47
group: Not lost | not cast out, nothing lost, none plucked from His hand | John 6:37; John 6:39; John 10:28-29; John 17:12
group: Washed, sealed and kept | clean every whit, sealed by the Spirit, kept by the power of God | John 13:10; John 17:11; Ephesians 1:13; 1 Peter 1:5
group: Raised and received | raised at the last day, received to Himself, with Him where He is | John 6:40; John 14:2-3; John 14:19; John 17:24
take: Life that could be lost would not be everlasting. The believer is kept by the Father’s will, the Son’s hand and the Son’s resurrection.

--- The literalist and the believer
lead: At every turn the literal hearer is shown to be wrong, and the believer looks past the figure to the One speaking.
parallel: The literalist | The believer
row: [[John 2:20]]“Forty and six years” | [[John 2:22]]They believed His word
row: [[John 3:4]]“Born when he is old?” | [[John 1:12-13]]Born of God
row: [[John 4:11]]“The well is deep” | [[John 4:42]]“The Saviour of the world”
row: [[John 6:52]]“How can this man…?” | [[John 6:68]]“Words of eternal life”
row: [[John 9:40]]“Are we blind also?” | [[John 9:38]]“Lord, I believe”
row: [[John 14:8]]“Shew us the Father” | [[John 16:30]]“Thou camest forth from God”
row: [[John 20:25]]“I will not believe” | [[John 20:28]]“My Lord and my God”
take: The literalist argues with the figure. The believer receives the word and understands.

--- The plain truth
- God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son.
- The Son is God. The Word was God, and Thomas called Him “My Lord and my God.”
- He died for our sins, was buried and rose again the third day.
- Whosoever believeth in Him should not perish, but have everlasting life, by grace through faith with nothing added.
- The believer is quickened and sealed by the Spirit and kept by the power of God.
- He spoke in figures and dark sayings that sorted His hearers.
- The literalist stumbled over the figure. The believer heard words that are spirit and life.
big: John 20:31
